From 4f82fd621704c707b9638647d3f11ae1db763346 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: whn09 <wanghenan09@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 12:14:37 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 05/12] vdn: data-parallel video VAE decode across the Ulysses
 ranks

The decode is the bigger half of a 480p render's post-warmup latency -- 13.8 s against
8.9 s of denoising -- and upstream runs all of it on rank 0 while seven cards wait at the
barrier.

It parallelises almost for free, because of what the decoder is. Only the ENCODER is a
causal 3D CNN; the decoder is a non-causal ViT, so no conv cache is threaded from one
temporal chunk to the next and `_decode_clip` is a pure function of its 7-latent-frame
slice. Spatial tiles inside a clip are independent too, and the only coupling anywhere is
`_blend` -- a linear cross-fade in pixel space, after every forward has finished. At 345
frames that is a grid of 20 chunks x 15 tiles = 300 independent decoder calls at 480p (28
tiles, 560 at 768p), and the measured 768p/480p decode ratio of 1.82x against the 1.87x
call ratio says the call count is the cost.

That makes it data parallelism, not tensor parallelism: TP would shard a 2048-wide ViT and
pay an all-reduce inside each of the 300 tiles, DP pays one all-gather for the whole
decode. Chunks are the unit rather than (chunk, tile) pairs -- 20/3 = 6.67x against
300/38 = 7.89x, but pairs ship overlapping tiles, 2.37x the pixels at 480p, for a fifth of
a second, and chunks leave upstream's tile stitching untouched.

Output is bit-identical: the same slices through the same `_decode_clip`, then `_decode`'s
own blend chain on rank 0.

Off by default (parallel.parallel_vae_decode): it needs the video VAE's weights on every
rank instead of only on rank 0. decode_video_latents is split out of decode_and_save
because the collective cannot live inside a rank-0-only branch.
